{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwPDxm88HzsmpoxmbYX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GG1fKeD7VA9pvL8YTizDQv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060643__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1986,"cache_read_input_tokens":26970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1986},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c80e66f-5c94-452e-938f-9b08d33b83c6","uuid":"e0417c2b-bfb0-4e97-93c6-0da04f53bec8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:06:50.600Z","request_id":"req_011CghwPDf9mbLFpox5V6mS8","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GG1fKeD7VA9pvL8YTizDQv":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060643__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":300}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GG1fKeD7VA9pvL8YTizDQv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Batzooka Baseball\n2\t\n3\t*The build plan for the over-the-top arcade baseball demo that TASK.md asks for. Read TASK.md first. This plan settles\n4\teverything TASK.md leaves open: the title, genre, cameras, architecture, every system, the content, the build order, the\n5\trisks and the tests. A capable developer (or model) should be able to build the whole demo from TASK.md and this file\n6\talone, without the planner.*\n7\t\n8\t**Title: BATZOOKA BASEBALL.** Picked the way TASK.md asks. Fifty playful riffs on \"baseball\" were brainstormed and\n9\tnumbered without being shown, then a real RNG drew the number (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`\n10\tprinted **37**: *Batzooka Baseball*). The name also gives the game its signature mechanic: the **Batzooka**, a\n11\trocket-powered bat that the crowd's roar charges up.\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 0. At a glance\n16\t\n17\t| | |\n18\t|---|---|\n19\t| Game | **Batzooka Baseball**, an original over-the-top arcade baseball game |\n20\t| Pitch | Pitch fireballs, swing a rocket-powered bat, rob homers by climbing the wall of a moon dome, and empty the benches in slapstick dust clouds, on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark from a mega-corporation. |\n21\t| Core loop | Win the duel at the plate (pitch or swing on timing and aim), then survive the chaos after contact (field, throw, run, slide), while big plays charge the Batzooka meter that unlocks supers. |\n22\t| Genre | Arcade sports (baseball), plus a slapstick brawler mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light upgrades |\n23\t| Perspective | **Plate Cam**: a pseudo-3D Mode 7 view from behind the catcher for every pitch and swing. **Broadcast Cam**: a three-quarter view of the park for fielding and baserunning. **Moonshot Cam**: a Mode 7 chase camera for home runs. Replays use a turned view. |\n24\t| Players | 1–4 on one machine (versus or co-op); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (a ghost-mode Derby round) |\n25\t| Modes | Tour (story: 5 road games and a boss), Exhibition, Batzooka Derby (home-run derby), Spring Training (tutorial); plus attract mode and dev mode |\n26\t| Engine | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package, full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, every module), with the small documented changes in §3.3 |\n27\t| Look | The engine's native look: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; textured TileMap ballparks; lit night games; particles everywhere |\n28\t| Audio | The engine's chip synth only: original music, sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices. No audio files. |\n29\t\n30\t**Exact deliverable names.** In file names, TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]` is written with GameTitle = `BatzookaBaseball` and SOURCE = `Baseball`:\n31\t\n32\t- dev folder: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/` (inside this folder)\n33\t- the game, a single file: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819.html` (in this folder, beside PLAN.md)\n34\t- `exit-interview.json` (in this folder, beside the .html)\n35\t\n36\t**Traceability.** Where each TASK.md requirement is handled:\n37\t\n38\t| TASK.md asks for | Where |\n39\t|---|---|\n40\t| Genre and perspective | §2, §6.12 |\n41\t| Title by RNG | top of this file |\n42\t| Single HTML package | §12 |\n43\t| Built on the handoff engine; engine may change | §3, §4 |\n44\t| Art direction (engine style plus new baseball assets) | §8 |\n45\t| Animation (engine rigs plus new baseball motion) | §8.3 |\n46\t| Audio in code; Animalese voices | §6.15, §6.16 |\n47\t| Online: AI Hub `lib/hub` | §6.21 |\n48\t| Multiplayer on its own | §6.20 |\n49\t| Dev folder, bundling, shared machine (ports, processes) | §4.1, §11.1, §12 |\n50\t| Feature-complete vertical slice; infrastructure for the rest | §1, §6, §7, §9 |\n51\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | §6.23 |\n52\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle, any input exits | §6.22 |\n53\t| Narrative tone in the source's style | §6.15, §7.5, §7.9 |\n54\t| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; glyphs follow the last device; Xbox option | §6.13, §6.14, §6.24 |\n55\t| Touch-only devices | §6.26 |\n56\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §12 |\n57\t| Source Information and feedback in the chat reply and an About/Credits screen | §6.27, §12 |\n58\t| exit-interview.json | §6.27, §12 |\n59\t| Testing: headless, one command, scripted playthrough | §11 |\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t## 1. Design pillars\n64\t\n65\t1. **The duel at the plate.** Baseball is a duel of timing and guesswork. Every pitch is seen from the Plate Cam: the\n66\t   pitcher picks a pitch and spot and times the release, then steers the ball a little in flight. The batter reads it,\n67\t   aims the sweet spot and times the swing. The result comes straight from timing and aim, so it is readable and fair.\n68\t2. **Chaos after contact.** At the crack of the bat the camera cuts wide. The ball becomes the star: fielders chase its\n69\t   shadow, dive, climb walls and fire throws at the bases, while runners turbo, slide and bowl over catchers.\n70\t3. **Baseball's texture.** The count, the diamond, the chalk lines, the organ, the PA voice echoing names, the crowd's\n71\t   roar and groan, the play-by-play duo, vendors, mascots, the between-innings show. It must feel like a day (or a\n72\t   night) at the ballpark.\n73\t4. **Over the top.** Every system has a turned-to-eleven version: pitches that catch fire, vanish or zig-zag; a bat\n74\t   that fires the ball like a rocket; fielders who climb walls and throw lasers; benches that empty into cartoon dust\n75\t   clouds; parks on a volcano, by the sea, on the moon, in a graveyard and in a corporate megadome.\n76\t5. **Ten feet away, one controller.** Big, bold and few things on screen at once. Every prompt shows the button on the\n77\t   device used last. Keyboard and mouse feel native: the mouse aims the bat and the pitch and clicks bases to throw.\n78\t6. **Readable rules.** Real baseball where it is fun, arcade-simple where it is fussy. §6.1 lists the rules kept,\n79\t   simplified and dropped, and Spring Training teaches what the player can and cannot do.\n80\t\n81\t**Tone.** The voice of old-time radio play-by-play turned up to arcade volume: tall tales, baseball slang, warm\n82\thyperbole and running gags. It is never mean, and its violence is cartoon slapstick (§6.15).\n83\t\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t## 2. Genre and perspective\n87\t\n88\t**Genre: arcade baseball.** The source is baseball \"over the top, arcade style\", so the game is a full arcade baseball\n89\tgame: real innings, counts, outs and runs, with exaggerated physics, super moves and a meter. Two other genres are woven\n90\tin to carry the over-the-top tone:\n91\t- a **brawler mini-game** (the Rhubarb), a 12-second bench-clearing scrum built on the engine's beat-'em-up tools;\n92\t- a **story Tour** with light RPG progression (Fan Bucks, team upgrades, rewards from beaten rivals), which gives the\n93\t  demo a beginning, a middle, an end and a boss.\n94\t\n95\t**Perspective: two main cameras over one world.** Every great arcade baseball game splits the screen's job: the duel\n96\tis seen from behind the plate, the play after contact from up high. Both cameras draw the same world (the same TileMap\n97\tand world coordinates), so cutting between them is seamless.\n98\t\n99\t| Camera | When | Built with | Why |\n100\t|---|---|---|---|\n101\t| **Plate Cam** | every pitch, swing, bunt, steal start and pickoff | `E.Mode7` (the mode7 module): a perspective floor (the park's own TileMap floor), the park's Backdrop as the sky, rigs as billboards scaled by distance, and walls, stands and the ball drawn with `m7.add` | The iconic baseball framing. The pitch grows as it comes in, which is what makes timing a swing feel right. Mode 7 is the engine's native perspective view. |\n102\t| **Broadcast Cam** | ball in play: fielding, throws, baserunning, rundowns, the Rhubarb, cutscenes | a custom ground view, registered as `E.VIEWS.broadcast = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 52, 1, 1)` the way the pinball kit registers its table view, over the park TileMap; `cam.frame` on the ball, its landing marker and the chaser | The engine's richest look (lit TileMap, shadows, props, particles, crowd). Home plate sits at the bottom of the screen, so up on the stick is toward center field. |\n103\t| **Moonshot Cam** | home runs and Batzooka blasts | Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball)` in slow motion, then a stands shot and the trot in Broadcast | The spectacle beat |\n104\t| **Replay Cam** | after big plays (optional, skippable) | Broadcast data replayed in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45) at half speed | A second angle on the highlight |\n105\t\n106\tThe Broadcast Cam also has the player options **High** (`E.VIEWS.high = new E.View('high', 'High', 0, 70, 1, 1)`) and **Isometric** (the engine's\n107\t`iso`), in Settings and on R3 / V.\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## 3. Tech stack and engine\n112\t\n113\t### 3.1 What to fetch (once, in M0)\n114\t\n115\tCopy everything into the dev folder; never edit anything outside it.\n116\t\n117\t- **The engine handoff package**, from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`: a shallow\n118\t  clone into its own temporary folder (or raw files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`).\n119\t  Take `my-3d2dge.js` (the full engine, every module), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, the\n120\t  `tools/` folder (check, filmstrip, replay, asset-sheet, asset-find, asset-lib, asset-set), `package.json` (pins\n121\t  Playwright 1.63.0), and `kits/sports.js` and `kits/kart.js` to read (ball and formation code; Mode 7 rigs).\n122\t- **The motion clips**, from the same repository (they are not in the handoff): `src/mocap/readable.js`,\n123\t  `src/mocap/mocap.js`, `tools/anim-set.mjs` with `tools/mocap-lib.mjs`, `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_124.js` (CMU subject\n124\t  124: *Baseball Pitch* 124_01 and 124_02, *Baseball Swing* 124_07, *Baseball Bunt* 124_08, *Underhand Toss* 124_12,\n125\t  *Underhand Fast Pitch* 124_13, *2 Foot Jump* 124_11), `src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js` (`Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n126\t  `Slide_Exit`, `Punch_Jab`, `Punch_Cross`), `src/mocap/sets/mesh2motion.js` (`Victory`, `Cheering_Two_Hands`,\n127\t  `Cheer_One_arm`), and `docs/MOCAP.md` (read its usage parts).\n128\t- **The AI Hub client and harness**: only these files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`:\n129\t  `README.md` (the protocol), `session.js` (client 1.4.0; pasted **unmodified**), `harness.html`, `bridge.js`,\n130\t  `round.js`. Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is over 5 GB.\n131\t\n132\t### 3.2 Engine features to use (don't reinvent them)\n133\t\n134\t`new E.Game({ res: 'ps1', expand: true, menu: {...} })` · scenes with `pausable`, `view`, `input`, `camera`, `touch` ·\n135\tboot steps (`game.start({ boot })`) · `E.TileMap` (floor tags, `floorTex`, wall `types` with `face`, `roof`, `cut`) ·\n136\t`E.Body` for players · `E.Humanoid` (builds, outfits, hats, `colors`, poses, `expr`, `attack` specs, `drawPortrait`) ·\n137\t`E.Blob` for mascots, gulls and ghosts · `E.Mode7` · `E.Backdrop` · `r.shape` / `E.SHAPES` / `map.solid` · `r.prop`\n138\t(lamps, street lamps, banners, torches, braziers, fire drums, lanterns) · `game.lights` · `game.particles` and `game.hitFx` · `game.weather` (ash,\n139\tleaves, rain) · `game.flash`, `shake`, `freeze`, `timeScale` · `game.tween`, `game.script`, `E.Path` (blimp, drones,\n140\tgulls, camera moves) · `E.arc` (throws) · `E.rng` (seeded streams) · `E.Dialog` (cutscenes, radio chatter, portraits) ·\n141\t`E.Menu` · `E.Arcade` (high-score tables and the initials letter wheel) · `game.saves` · `game.settings` and\n142\t`settings.define` (sliders, selects, toggles) · `game.input` (custom preset, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`,\n143\t`allowJoin`, `addPlayer`, `halfKeys`, `rumble`, `suppressCode`, `clicked`, `pointer`, `mouseGround`) · `game.audio`\n144\t(`define`, `sfx`, `music` with named layers, `sting`, `tempo`, `duck`) · `E.Combo`, `E.Attack`, `E.knockback` (the\n145\tRhubarb) · `E.font.title`, `E.ui.box`, `E.ui.bar` · `game.debug`. Motion clips go through `Mocap.load`, `Mocap.drive`,\n146\t`lib.sample`, `rig.mocapW`, `rig.mocapMask` and `MocapReadable.mirror`.\n147\t\n148\t**Already built in; don't rebuild:** the pause menu, the settings screens, control rebinding (with conflict checks),\n149\tbutton-symbol families, full screen, the corner menu button, pausing when focus is lost or a pad is unplugged, the boot\n150\tscreen and crash report, save slots, the comfort settings (reduce motion, shake, flashes) and screen filters (CRT and\n151\tscanlines suit an arcade game).\n152\t\n153\t### 3.3 Engine changes (in the dev folder's copy; list each in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` and in the About screen's engine feedback)\n154\t\n155\t1. **`weapon: 'bat'`** on `E.Humanoid`: add it to the built-in list (the warning at `this.o.weapon && ![...]`), a rest\n156\t   direction (bat up over the back shoulder), a draw branch beside `'staff'`, and `tip()` = the barrel's end. The bat is\n157\t   a tapered capsule: knob, thin handle, thick barrel, in `colors.bat` (ash `#c89a5a`; the Batzooka is drawn by the\n158\t   game over it). With `grip: 'both'`, IK the left hand to the handle 2.5 U below the right after every\n159\t   `solvePose()`, so swings and bunts hold the bat in two hands.\n160\t2. **`mitt`** option: `'fielder' | 'catcher' | 'first' | null`, a leather mitt on the glove hand (`handL` for\n161\t   right-handed throwers, `handR` for lefties): a pocketed oval about 2.2× the hand's size, in `colors.mitt`. The\n162\t   catcher's mitt is rounder and bigger.\n163\t3. **Hats:** `hat: 'batting'` (a glossy helmet with a brim and an ear flap on the side facing the pitcher), `hat: 'mask'`\n164\t   (a catcher's helmet with a cage of 3 bars over the face), and `hat: 'visor'` (a space helmet for the moon team: a\n165\t   dome with a tinted front) and `hat: 'tophat'` (Mortimer Megamax's tall silk hat). Portraits draw them too.\n166\t4. **Font:** add the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 font, so any `input.label()` text renders (the in-game prompts still use\n167\t   the icon renderer, §6.13).\n168\t5. **The standard `glyphs` setting** (group Controller, \"Button symbols\"): default `'playstation'`; options\n169\t   `[['playstation', 'PlayStation (✕ ○ □ △)'], ['xbox', 'Xbox (A B X Y)']]`. A DualSense or any standard pad then shows\n170\t   PlayStation symbols unless the player picks Xbox.\n171\t6. Only if Mode 7 needs it: a helper `m7.quad(pts4, color, depth)` that draws a filled world-space quad sorted with\n172\t   the billboards (the fence and the stand faces). It can live in game code over `m7.add` instead.\n173\t7. Fix any engine bug the tests uncover, with a line on what and why.\n174\t\n175\tKeep every change small and commented `// BATZOOKA:`. Nothing in the game may depend on code outside the dev folder.\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## 4. Architecture\n180\t\n181\t### 4.1 Dev folder layout\n182\t\n183\t```\n184\tBatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/\n185\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js          the engine: handoff v0.85.2 plus the changes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n186\t  engine/mocap-readable.js     the repo's src/mocap/readable.js (unchanged)\n187\t  engine/mocap.js              the repo's src/mocap/mocap.js (unchanged)\n188\t  assets/mocap-batzooka.js     the clip set cut by tools/anim-set.mjs (§8.3)\n189\t  vendor/aihub-session.js      AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n190\t  src/*.js                     the game: files concatenated by name into one IIFE (§4.2)\n191\t  build/game.js                the concatenated game (written by tools/build.mjs; dev page and tests load it)\n192\t  index.html                   dev page: engine, mocap, vendor client, build/game.js as separate scripts\n193\t  tools/build.mjs              src → build/game.js; then the single-file bundle → ../<name>.html\n194\t  tools/serve.mjs              a static server on 127.0.0.1, port 0 (prints the port; for the hub harness)\n195\t  tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-sheet.mjs, asset-find.mjs, asset-lib.mjs, asset-set.mjs (handoff)\n196\t  tools/anim-set.mjs, mocap-lib.mjs (repo)\n197\t  hub/harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md (AI Hub)\n198\t  tests/run.mjs                the one command (§11.1)\n199\t  tests/lib/*.mjs              the headless loader, assertions, bots\n200\t  tests/*.test.mjs             the tests (§11.3)\n201\t  package.json                 { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" }, scripts: { test, build, check } }\n202\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md            each engine change and why\n203\t  NOTES.md                     tuning notes, decisions, the feedback answers as they come\n204\t```\n205\t\n206\t### 4.2 Source files\n207\t\n208\t`tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/*.js` in name order inside one `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })();`.\n209\tEach file adds to one namespace object, `BZ`. Files never reach into each other's locals.\n210\t\n211\t| File | Holds |\n212\t|---|---|\n213\t| `00-core.js` | `BZ`; `BZ.def(kind, id, spec)` and `BZ.get(kind, id)` registries; the event bus `BZ.bus` (`on`, `off`, `emit`, a ring buffer of the last 200 events); RNG streams `BZ.rng.play / fx / deal`; `BZ.TUNE`, `BZ.TUNE_META`, `BZ.resetTune()`; unit helpers (`feet(u)`, `mph(uPerS)`, angle helpers); constants |\n214\t| `01-input.js` | the action preset; input contexts and routing; device tracking; the glyph icon renderer and prompt builder; mouse helpers (pointer → strike-zone plane, pointer → ground → base); touch-only detection |\n215\t| `02-audio.js` | SFX definitions; the crowd bed; the music director (song per scene, layers, stings, tempo); the **Babble** voice synth |\n216\t| `03-rigs.js` | player rig factory (team uniform + player looks); custom poses and attack specs (swing, bunt, windup, throw, tag, dive, climb, umpire calls); the mocap driver (clip windows, mirrored clips for lefties, blending); Batzooka and mitt overdraw |\n217\t| `04-shapes.js` | `E.SHAPES` entries for ballpark objects (§7.12) |\n218\t| `05-park.js` | the park builder: geometry from a park def → TileMap rows, legend, types and `floorTex`; fence function, foul lines and bases; stand seats; light rigs; props; the Plate Cam's Mode 7 floor, sky and world billboards |\n219\t| `10-rules.js` | the rules engine, pure logic: count, outs, bases, forces, scoring, half-innings, game end, extra innings, mercy rule; line score and box stats |\n220\t| `11-pitch.js` | pitch catalog; selection; windup and release ring; flight paths; aftertouch; stamina; calls; the catcher's catch and passed balls |\n221\t| `12-bat.js` | the sweet-spot reticle; swing types; the contact model; bunts; contact → launch |\n222\t| `13-ball.js` | batted and thrown ball physics; prediction; fence, pole, stands and foul tests; home runs; ground-rule doubles; wind |\n223\t| `14-field.js` | fielders: positions, roles, assignment, chase, catch, dive, jump, wall climb, throws, cutoffs, tags, forces, rundowns |\n224\t| `15-run.js` | baserunners: the batter-runner, forces, tag-ups, advance and retreat orders, steals, leads, pickoffs, slides, bowl-overs, scoring |\n225\t| `16-supers.js` | the Batzooka meter, the supers (pitch, bat, throw, run), turbo |\n226\t| `17-rhubarb.js` | the bench-clearing brawl |\n227\t| `18-hazards.js` | the six parks' hazards and features |\n228\t| `20-ai.js` | CPU pitcher, batter, fielders and runners by difficulty; **the bot**, which turns a CPU brain's choices into player inputs (for tests, the playthrough and the attract mode) |\n229\t| `21-cams.js` | Plate Cam, Broadcast Cam, Moonshot Cam, cut-ins, the replay ring buffer, transitions |\n230\t| `22-hud.js` | scorebug, count, bases, meters, player cards, prompts, call stamps, radar, radio box, pitch and timing feedback |\n231\t| `23-talk.js` | the commentary director (line pools, priorities, rate limit, variables), the PA and the umpire; the cutscene runner |\n232\t| `24-crowd.js` | crowd seats and animation; hype; the wave; the jumbotron; the mascot; fireworks; between-innings shows |\n233\t| `30-match.js` | the match scene: its phases, update order and draw order |\n234\t| `31-derby.js` | the Derby scene (local hot-seat and hub) |\n235\t| `32-training.js` | Spring Training lessons |\n236\t| `33-tour.js` | Tour map, clubhouse, story flow, rewards, saves |\n237\t| `34-menus.js` | title (with its mode menu), team select and joining, results, Playbook (help), About/Credits, pause items |\n238\t| `35-attract.js` | the attract director and its segments |\n239\t| `36-dev.js` | the dev mode overlay |\n240\t| `40-hub.js` | AI Hub session flow, its card, ghosts |\n241\t| `50-teams.js` … `56-songs.js` | content data: teams, parks, story, lessons, lines, upgrades, songs |\n242\t| `90-start.js` | boot steps, the scenes table, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` |\n243\t\n244\t### 4.3 The spine (rules every module follows)\n245\t\n246\t- **One match state, `BZ.M`** (fields in §6.1). The simulation runs only in `update(dt)` (exact 1/120 s steps); `draw`\n247\t  only reads state. A whole match can run headless with `draw` skipped.\n248\t- **Seeded gameplay.** Gameplay randomness comes only from `BZ.rng.play` (`E.rng(seed)`, re-seeded per match;\n249\t  `game.debug.setup({ seed })`). The Derby's pitch sequence comes from `BZ.rng.deal`, which is `AIHub.rng('pitches')` in\n250\t  a hub session. Cosmetic randomness (particles, crowd, babble variation) uses `BZ.rng.fx` or the engine's own. Rules,\n251\t  pitches, contact and AI never call `Math.random`.\n252\t- **Everything is an event.** Each outcome is emitted on the bus (`BZ.bus.emit('homer', { batter, dist, batzooka })`).\n253\t  Commentary, crowd, audio, HUD stamps, stats, meter gains, the hub reporter, the attract director and the tests all\n254\t  listen. A new listener never edits the rules. The events are listed in Appendix C.\n255\t- **Content is data.** Teams, players, parks, pitches, supers, hazards, lessons, story scenes, line pools, upgrades and\n256\t  songs are registry entries (`BZ.def('parks', 'caldera', {...})`), referenced by id. Adding a park or a team is a data\n257\t  entry, plus a hazard plug-in if it has one. Together with dev mode (live tuning, situations, warps, the gallery)\n258\t  and the seeded sims, these data files are the \"normal designer tools\" TASK.md asks for: the rest of the game can be\n259\t  built out by adding entries, not code.\n260\t- **Live tuning.** Every balance number lives in `BZ.TUNE` and is described in `BZ.TUNE_META` (label, group, min, max,\n261\t  step, default; Appendix A). Code reads `TUNE.x` each time it needs it and never caches it, so dev sliders act at once.\n262\t- **Rigs are cosmetic.** Gameplay never reads rig joints: the ball's release and contact points come from fixed\n263\t  offsets per player and job (the gallery checks that they match the drawn hand and bat). So headless sims, tests and\n264\t  the playthrough can skip `rig.update` (`BZ.cosmetic = false`), which keeps thousands of simulated innings fast.\n265\t- **Input through contexts.** Modules ask the context router (§6.14), never raw keys.\n266\t- **One debug handle.** `game.debug` serves the tests, the dev mode and the attract director (§11.2).\n267\t\n268\t### 4.4 Scenes and flow\n269\t\n270\t```\n271\tboot ──(AIHub.active and connect() gives info)──► hubDerby: card → countdown → round → end overlay\n272\t  └──► title ◄──────► attract   (after 10 s with no input on title or top menus; any input returns)\n273\t         ├─ TOUR ──► tourMenu (Continue · New · Load) ─► tourMap ⇄ clubhouse\n274\t         │             tourMap ─► story ─► match ─► results ─► story ─► tourMap … final ─► ending ─► credits ─► title\n275\t         ├─ EXHIBITION ─► teamSelect (join 1–4, sides, teams, park, innings, difficulty) ─► match ─► results\n276\t         ├─ DERBY ─► derbySetup (1–4 players, park, length) ─► derby ─► derbyResults (initials, table)\n277\t         ├─ SPRING TRAINING ─► lessonList ─► lesson ─► lessonList\n278\t         ├─ OPTIONS ─► game.menu('settings') (the engine's screens, with ours added)\n279\t         └─ ABOUT ─► about (About · Source Information · Feedback · Credits)\n280\t\n281\tpause menu, on every pausable scene: Resume · Playbook · Pitching Change* · Restart* · Dev Mode* · Settings · Controls ·\n282\tFull screen · Quit to title*      (* hidden in AI Hub sessions; Pitching Change only on defense in a match)\n283\t```\n284\t\n285\tDeep links for tests and checks: `#title`, `#attract`, `#match` (optionally `#match/<park>`), `#derby`, `#training/<n>`,\n286\t`#tour/<stop>`, `#about`, `#gallery` (the animation gallery), `#hubcard` (the hub card with a mock), `#touch`\n287\t(forces the touch-only title).\n288\t\n289\t`menu.items` is given as a function that returns entries, with falsy ones skipped. The engine's pause screen evaluates\n290\tit each time it opens (engine line ~9470: `v(this._game.o.menu.items)` then `.filter(Boolean)`; confirm this in M0 for\n291\tthe standard menu).\n292\t\n293\t---\n294\t\n295\t## 5. The ballpark: units and geometry\n296\t\n297\t**Units.** 16 U (world units) is one tile. Players are `size: 1.25` rigs, about 33 U tall. The game shows distances in\n298\tfeet (`feet = U × 0.32`) and speeds in mph (`mph = U/s × 0.26`). Proportions are arcade, not real: the basepath shows as\n299\t96 ft and center field about 380 ft, which reads right.\n300\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c80e66f-5c94-452e-938f-9b08d33b83c6","uuid":"adb02d75-4b29-45fd-a2af-6a2790484157","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:06:50.607Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060643__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Batzooka Baseball\n\n*The build plan for the over-the-top arcade baseball demo that TASK.md asks for. Read TASK.md first. This plan settles\neverything TASK.md leaves open: the title, genre, cameras, architecture, every system, the content, the build order, the\nrisks and the tests. A capable developer (or model) should be able to build the whole demo from TASK.md and this file\nalone, without the planner.*\n\n**Title: BATZOOKA BASEBALL.** Picked the way TASK.md asks. Fifty playful riffs on \"baseball\" were brainstormed and\nnumbered without being shown, then a real RNG drew the number (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`\nprinted **37**: *Batzooka Baseball*). The name also gives the game its signature mechanic: the **Batzooka**, a\nrocket-powered bat that the crowd's roar charges up.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Game | **Batzooka Baseball**, an original over-the-top arcade baseball game |\n| Pitch | Pitch fireballs, swing a rocket-powered bat, rob homers by climbing the wall of a moon dome, and empty the benches in slapstick dust clouds, on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark from a mega-corporation. |\n| Core loop | Win the duel at the plate (pitch or swing on timing and aim), then survive the chaos after contact (field, throw, run, slide), while big plays charge the Batzooka meter that unlocks supers. |\n| Genre | Arcade sports (baseball), plus a slapstick brawler mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light upgrades |\n| Perspective | **Plate Cam**: a pseudo-3D Mode 7 view from behind the catcher for every pitch and swing. **Broadcast Cam**: a three-quarter view of the park for fielding and baserunning. **Moonshot Cam**: a Mode 7 chase camera for home runs. Replays use a turned view. |\n| Players | 1–4 on one machine (versus or co-op); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (a ghost-mode Derby round) |\n| Modes | Tour (story: 5 road games and a boss), Exhibition, Batzooka Derby (home-run derby), Spring Training (tutorial); plus attract mode and dev mode |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package, full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, every module), with the small documented changes in §3.3 |\n| Look | The engine's native look: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; textured TileMap ballparks; lit night games; particles everywhere |\n| Audio | The engine's chip synth only: original music, sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices. No audio files. |\n\n**Exact deliverable names.** In file names, TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]` is written with GameTitle = `BatzookaBaseball` and SOURCE = `Baseball`:\n\n- dev folder: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/` (inside this folder)\n- the game, a single file: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819.html` (in this folder, beside PLAN.md)\n- `exit-interview.json` (in this folder, beside the .html)\n\n**Traceability.** Where each TASK.md requirement is handled:\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective | §2, §6.12 |\n| Title by RNG | top of this file |\n| Single HTML package | §12 |\n| Built on the handoff engine; engine may change | §3, §4 |\n| Art direction (engine style plus new baseball assets) | §8 |\n| Animation (engine rigs plus new baseball motion) | §8.3 |\n| Audio in code; Animalese voices | §6.15, §6.16 |\n| Online: AI Hub `lib/hub` | §6.21 |\n| Multiplayer on its own | §6.20 |\n| Dev folder, bundling, shared machine (ports, processes) | §4.1, §11.1, §12 |\n| Feature-complete vertical slice; infrastructure for the rest | §1, §6, §7, §9 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | §6.23 |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle, any input exits | §6.22 |\n| Narrative tone in the source's style | §6.15, §7.5, §7.9 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; glyphs follow the last device; Xbox option | §6.13, §6.14, §6.24 |\n| Touch-only devices | §6.26 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §12 |\n| Source Information and feedback in the chat reply and an About/Credits screen | §6.27, §12 |\n| exit-interview.json | §6.27, §12 |\n| Testing: headless, one command, scripted playthrough | §11 |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design pillars\n\n1. **The duel at the plate.** Baseball is a duel of timing and guesswork. Every pitch is seen from the Plate Cam: the\n   pitcher picks a pitch and spot and times the release, then steers the ball a little in flight. The batter reads it,\n   aims the sweet spot and times the swing. The result comes straight from timing and aim, so it is readable and fair.\n2. **Chaos after contact.** At the crack of the bat the camera cuts wide. The ball becomes the star: fielders chase its\n   shadow, dive, climb walls and fire throws at the bases, while runners turbo, slide and bowl over catchers.\n3. **Baseball's texture.** The count, the diamond, the chalk lines, the organ, the PA voice echoing names, the crowd's\n   roar and groan, the play-by-play duo, vendors, mascots, the between-innings show. It must feel like a day (or a\n   night) at the ballpark.\n4. **Over the top.** Every system has a turned-to-eleven version: pitches that catch fire, vanish or zig-zag; a bat\n   that fires the ball like a rocket; fielders who climb walls and throw lasers; benches that empty into cartoon dust\n   clouds; parks on a volcano, by the sea, on the moon, in a graveyard and in a corporate megadome.\n5. **Ten feet away, one controller.** Big, bold and few things on screen at once. Every prompt shows the button on the\n   device used last. Keyboard and mouse feel native: the mouse aims the bat and the pitch and clicks bases to throw.\n6. **Readable rules.** Real baseball where it is fun, arcade-simple where it is fussy. §6.1 lists the rules kept,\n   simplified and dropped, and Spring Training teaches what the player can and cannot do.\n\n**Tone.** The voice of old-time radio play-by-play turned up to arcade volume: tall tales, baseball slang, warm\nhyperbole and running gags. It is never mean, and its violence is cartoon slapstick (§6.15).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Genre and perspective\n\n**Genre: arcade baseball.** The source is baseball \"over the top, arcade style\", so the game is a full arcade baseball\ngame: real innings, counts, outs and runs, with exaggerated physics, super moves and a meter. Two other genres are woven\nin to carry the over-the-top tone:\n- a **brawler mini-game** (the Rhubarb), a 12-second bench-clearing scrum built on the engine's beat-'em-up tools;\n- a **story Tour** with light RPG progression (Fan Bucks, team upgrades, rewards from beaten rivals), which gives the\n  demo a beginning, a middle, an end and a boss.\n\n**Perspective: two main cameras over one world.** Every great arcade baseball game splits the screen's job: the duel\nis seen from behind the plate, the play after contact from up high. Both cameras draw the same world (the same TileMap\nand world coordinates), so cutting between them is seamless.\n\n| Camera | When | Built with | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Plate Cam** | every pitch, swing, bunt, steal start and pickoff | `E.Mode7` (the mode7 module): a perspective floor (the park's own TileMap floor), the park's Backdrop as the sky, rigs as billboards scaled by distance, and walls, stands and the ball drawn with `m7.add` | The iconic baseball framing. The pitch grows as it comes in, which is what makes timing a swing feel right. Mode 7 is the engine's native perspective view. |\n| **Broadcast Cam** | ball in play: fielding, throws, baserunning, rundowns, the Rhubarb, cutscenes | a custom ground view, registered as `E.VIEWS.broadcast = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 52, 1, 1)` the way the pinball kit registers its table view, over the park TileMap; `cam.frame` on the ball, its landing marker and the chaser | The engine's richest look (lit TileMap, shadows, props, particles, crowd). Home plate sits at the bottom of the screen, so up on the stick is toward center field. |\n| **Moonshot Cam** | home runs and Batzooka blasts | Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball)` in slow motion, then a stands shot and the trot in Broadcast | The spectacle beat |\n| **Replay Cam** | after big plays (optional, skippable) | Broadcast data replayed in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45) at half speed | A second angle on the highlight |\n\nThe Broadcast Cam also has the player options **High** (`E.VIEWS.high = new E.View('high', 'High', 0, 70, 1, 1)`) and **Isometric** (the engine's\n`iso`), in Settings and on R3 / V.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and engine\n\n### 3.1 What to fetch (once, in M0)\n\nCopy everything into the dev folder; never edit anything outside it.\n\n- **The engine handoff package**, from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`: a shallow\n  clone into its own temporary folder (or raw files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`).\n  Take `my-3d2dge.js` (the full engine, every module), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, the\n  `tools/` folder (check, filmstrip, replay, asset-sheet, asset-find, asset-lib, asset-set), `package.json` (pins\n  Playwright 1.63.0), and `kits/sports.js` and `kits/kart.js` to read (ball and formation code; Mode 7 rigs).\n- **The motion clips**, from the same repository (they are not in the handoff): `src/mocap/readable.js`,\n  `src/mocap/mocap.js`, `tools/anim-set.mjs` with `tools/mocap-lib.mjs`, `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_124.js` (CMU subject\n  124: *Baseball Pitch* 124_01 and 124_02, *Baseball Swing* 124_07, *Baseball Bunt* 124_08, *Underhand Toss* 124_12,\n  *Underhand Fast Pitch* 124_13, *2 Foot Jump* 124_11), `src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js` (`Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n  `Slide_Exit`, `Punch_Jab`, `Punch_Cross`), `src/mocap/sets/mesh2motion.js` (`Victory`, `Cheering_Two_Hands`,\n  `Cheer_One_arm`), and `docs/MOCAP.md` (read its usage parts).\n- **The AI Hub client and harness**: only these files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`:\n  `README.md` (the protocol), `session.js` (client 1.4.0; pasted **unmodified**), `harness.html`, `bridge.js`,\n  `round.js`. Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is over 5 GB.\n\n### 3.2 Engine features to use (don't reinvent them)\n\n`new E.Game({ res: 'ps1', expand: true, menu: {...} })` · scenes with `pausable`, `view`, `input`, `camera`, `touch` ·\nboot steps (`game.start({ boot })`) · `E.TileMap` (floor tags, `floorTex`, wall `types` with `face`, `roof`, `cut`) ·\n`E.Body` for players · `E.Humanoid` (builds, outfits, hats, `colors`, poses, `expr`, `attack` specs, `drawPortrait`) ·\n`E.Blob` for mascots, gulls and ghosts · `E.Mode7` · `E.Backdrop` · `r.shape` / `E.SHAPES` / `map.solid` · `r.prop`\n(lamps, street lamps, banners, torches, braziers, fire drums, lanterns) · `game.lights` · `game.particles` and `game.hitFx` · `game.weather` (ash,\nleaves, rain) · `game.flash`, `shake`, `freeze`, `timeScale` · `game.tween`, `game.script`, `E.Path` (blimp, drones,\ngulls, camera moves) · `E.arc` (throws) · `E.rng` (seeded streams) · `E.Dialog` (cutscenes, radio chatter, portraits) ·\n`E.Menu` · `E.Arcade` (high-score tables and the initials letter wheel) · `game.saves` · `game.settings` and\n`settings.define` (sliders, selects, toggles) · `game.input` (custom preset, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`,\n`allowJoin`, `addPlayer`, `halfKeys`, `rumble`, `suppressCode`, `clicked`, `pointer`, `mouseGround`) · `game.audio`\n(`define`, `sfx`, `music` with named layers, `sting`, `tempo`, `duck`) · `E.Combo`, `E.Attack`, `E.knockback` (the\nRhubarb) · `E.font.title`, `E.ui.box`, `E.ui.bar` · `game.debug`. Motion clips go through `Mocap.load`, `Mocap.drive`,\n`lib.sample`, `rig.mocapW`, `rig.mocapMask` and `MocapReadable.mirror`.\n\n**Already built in; don't rebuild:** the pause menu, the settings screens, control rebinding (with conflict checks),\nbutton-symbol families, full screen, the corner menu button, pausing when focus is lost or a pad is unplugged, the boot\nscreen and crash report, save slots, the comfort settings (reduce motion, shake, flashes) and screen filters (CRT and\nscanlines suit an arcade game).\n\n### 3.3 Engine changes (in the dev folder's copy; list each in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` and in the About screen's engine feedback)\n\n1. **`weapon: 'bat'`** on `E.Humanoid`: add it to the built-in list (the warning at `this.o.weapon && ![...]`), a rest\n   direction (bat up over the back shoulder), a draw branch beside `'staff'`, and `tip()` = the barrel's end. The bat is\n   a tapered capsule: knob, thin handle, thick barrel, in `colors.bat` (ash `#c89a5a`; the Batzooka is drawn by the\n   game over it). With `grip: 'both'`, IK the left hand to the handle 2.5 U below the right after every\n   `solvePose()`, so swings and bunts hold the bat in two hands.\n2. **`mitt`** option: `'fielder' | 'catcher' | 'first' | null`, a leather mitt on the glove hand (`handL` for\n   right-handed throwers, `handR` for lefties): a pocketed oval about 2.2× the hand's size, in `colors.mitt`. The\n   catcher's mitt is rounder and bigger.\n3. **Hats:** `hat: 'batting'` (a glossy helmet with a brim and an ear flap on the side facing the pitcher), `hat: 'mask'`\n   (a catcher's helmet with a cage of 3 bars over the face), and `hat: 'visor'` (a space helmet for the moon team: a\n   dome with a tinted front) and `hat: 'tophat'` (Mortimer Megamax's tall silk hat). Portraits draw them too.\n4. **Font:** add the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 font, so any `input.label()` text renders (the in-game prompts still use\n   the icon renderer, §6.13).\n5. **The standard `glyphs` setting** (group Controller, \"Button symbols\"): default `'playstation'`; options\n   `[['playstation', 'PlayStation (✕ ○ □ △)'], ['xbox', 'Xbox (A B X Y)']]`. A DualSense or any standard pad then shows\n   PlayStation symbols unless the player picks Xbox.\n6. Only if Mode 7 needs it: a helper `m7.quad(pts4, color, depth)` that draws a filled world-space quad sorted with\n   the billboards (the fence and the stand faces). It can live in game code over `m7.add` instead.\n7. Fix any engine bug the tests uncover, with a line on what and why.\n\nKeep every change small and commented `// BATZOOKA:`. Nothing in the game may depend on code outside the dev folder.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Dev folder layout\n\n```\nBatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js          the engine: handoff v0.85.2 plus the changes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n  engine/mocap-readable.js     the repo's src/mocap/readable.js (unchanged)\n  engine/mocap.js              the repo's src/mocap/mocap.js (unchanged)\n  assets/mocap-batzooka.js     the clip set cut by tools/anim-set.mjs (§8.3)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js      AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n  src/*.js                     the game: files concatenated by name into one IIFE (§4.2)\n  build/game.js                the concatenated game (written by tools/build.mjs; dev page and tests load it)\n  index.html                   dev page: engine, mocap, vendor client, build/game.js as separate scripts\n  tools/build.mjs              src → build/game.js; then the single-file bundle → ../<name>.html\n  tools/serve.mjs              a static server on 127.0.0.1, port 0 (prints the port; for the hub harness)\n  tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-sheet.mjs, asset-find.mjs, asset-lib.mjs, asset-set.mjs (handoff)\n  tools/anim-set.mjs, mocap-lib.mjs (repo)\n  hub/harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md (AI Hub)\n  tests/run.mjs                the one command (§11.1)\n  tests/lib/*.mjs              the headless loader, assertions, bots\n  tests/*.test.mjs             the tests (§11.3)\n  package.json                 { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" }, scripts: { test, build, check } }\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md            each engine change and why\n  NOTES.md                     tuning notes, decisions, the feedback answers as they come\n```\n\n### 4.2 Source files\n\n`tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/*.js` in name order inside one `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })();`.\nEach file adds to one namespace object, `BZ`. Files never reach into each other's locals.\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `BZ`; `BZ.def(kind, id, spec)` and `BZ.get(kind, id)` registries; the event bus `BZ.bus` (`on`, `off`, `emit`, a ring buffer of the last 200 events); RNG streams `BZ.rng.play / fx / deal`; `BZ.TUNE`, `BZ.TUNE_META`, `BZ.resetTune()`; unit helpers (`feet(u)`, `mph(uPerS)`, angle helpers); constants |\n| `01-input.js` | the action preset; input contexts and routing; device tracking; the glyph icon renderer and prompt builder; mouse helpers (pointer → strike-zone plane, pointer → ground → base); touch-only detection |\n| `02-audio.js` | SFX definitions; the crowd bed; the music director (song per scene, layers, stings, tempo); the **Babble** voice synth |\n| `03-rigs.js` | player rig factory (team uniform + player looks); custom poses and attack specs (swing, bunt, windup, throw, tag, dive, climb, umpire calls); the mocap driver (clip windows, mirrored clips for lefties, blending); Batzooka and mitt overdraw |\n| `04-shapes.js` | `E.SHAPES` entries for ballpark objects (§7.12) |\n| `05-park.js` | the park builder: geometry from a park def → TileMap rows, legend, types and `floorTex`; fence function, foul lines and bases; stand seats; light rigs; props; the Plate Cam's Mode 7 floor, sky and world billboards |\n| `10-rules.js` | the rules engine, pure logic: count, outs, bases, forces, scoring, half-innings, game end, extra innings, mercy rule; line score and box stats |\n| `11-pitch.js` | pitch catalog; selection; windup and release ring; flight paths; aftertouch; stamina; calls; the catcher's catch and passed balls |\n| `12-bat.js` | the sweet-spot reticle; swing types; the contact model; bunts; contact → launch |\n| `13-ball.js` | batted and thrown ball physics; prediction; fence, pole, stands and foul tests; home runs; ground-rule doubles; wind |\n| `14-field.js` | fielders: positions, roles, assignment, chase, catch, dive, jump, wall climb, throws, cutoffs, tags, forces, rundowns |\n| `15-run.js` | baserunners: the batter-runner, forces, tag-ups, advance and retreat orders, steals, leads, pickoffs, slides, bowl-overs, scoring |\n| `16-supers.js` | the Batzooka meter, the supers (pitch, bat, throw, run), turbo |\n| `17-rhubarb.js` | the bench-clearing brawl |\n| `18-hazards.js` | the six parks' hazards and features |\n| `20-ai.js` | CPU pitcher, batter, fielders and runners by difficulty; **the bot**, which turns a CPU brain's choices into player inputs (for tests, the playthrough and the attract mode) |\n| `21-cams.js` | Plate Cam, Broadcast Cam, Moonshot Cam, cut-ins, the replay ring buffer, transitions |\n| `22-hud.js` | scorebug, count, bases, meters, player cards, prompts, call stamps, radar, radio box, pitch and timing feedback |\n| `23-talk.js` | the commentary director (line pools, priorities, rate limit, variables), the PA and the umpire; the cutscene runner |\n| `24-crowd.js` | crowd seats and animation; hype; the wave; the jumbotron; the mascot; fireworks; between-innings shows |\n| `30-match.js` | the match scene: its phases, update order and draw order |\n| `31-derby.js` | the Derby scene (local hot-seat and hub) |\n| `32-training.js` | Spring Training lessons |\n| `33-tour.js` | Tour map, clubhouse, story flow, rewards, saves |\n| `34-menus.js` | title (with its mode menu), team select and joining, results, Playbook (help), About/Credits, pause items |\n| `35-attract.js` | the attract director and its segments |\n| `36-dev.js` | the dev mode overlay |\n| `40-hub.js` | AI Hub session flow, its card, ghosts |\n| `50-teams.js` … `56-songs.js` | content data: teams, parks, story, lessons, lines, upgrades, songs |\n| `90-start.js` | boot steps, the scenes table, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` |\n\n### 4.3 The spine (rules every module follows)\n\n- **One match state, `BZ.M`** (fields in §6.1). The simulation runs only in `update(dt)` (exact 1/120 s steps); `draw`\n  only reads state. A whole match can run headless with `draw` skipped.\n- **Seeded gameplay.** Gameplay randomness comes only from `BZ.rng.play` (`E.rng(seed)`, re-seeded per match;\n  `game.debug.setup({ seed })`). The Derby's pitch sequence comes from `BZ.rng.deal`, which is `AIHub.rng('pitches')` in\n  a hub session. Cosmetic randomness (particles, crowd, babble variation) uses `BZ.rng.fx` or the engine's own. Rules,\n  pitches, contact and AI never call `Math.random`.\n- **Everything is an event.** Each outcome is emitted on the bus (`BZ.bus.emit('homer', { batter, dist, batzooka })`).\n  Commentary, crowd, audio, HUD stamps, stats, meter gains, the hub reporter, the attract director and the tests all\n  listen. A new listener never edits the rules. The events are listed in Appendix C.\n- **Content is data.** Teams, players, parks, pitches, supers, hazards, lessons, story scenes, line pools, upgrades and\n  songs are registry entries (`BZ.def('parks', 'caldera', {...})`), referenced by id. Adding a park or a team is a data\n  entry, plus a hazard plug-in if it has one. Together with dev mode (live tuning, situations, warps, the gallery)\n  and the seeded sims, these data files are the \"normal designer tools\" TASK.md asks for: the rest of the game can be\n  built out by adding entries, not code.\n- **Live tuning.** Every balance number lives in `BZ.TUNE` and is described in `BZ.TUNE_META` (label, group, min, max,\n  step, default; Appendix A). Code reads `TUNE.x` each time it needs it and never caches it, so dev sliders act at once.\n- **Rigs are cosmetic.** Gameplay never reads rig joints: the ball's release and contact points come from fixed\n  offsets per player and job (the gallery checks that they match the drawn hand and bat). So headless sims, tests and\n  the playthrough can skip `rig.update` (`BZ.cosmetic = false`), which keeps thousands of simulated innings fast.\n- **Input through contexts.** Modules ask the context router (§6.14), never raw keys.\n- **One debug handle.** `game.debug` serves the tests, the dev mode and the attract director (§11.2).\n\n### 4.4 Scenes and flow\n\n```\nboot ──(AIHub.active and connect() gives info)──► hubDerby: card → countdown → round → end overlay\n  └──► title ◄──────► attract   (after 10 s with no input on title or top menus; any input returns)\n         ├─ TOUR ──► tourMenu (Continue · New · Load) ─► tourMap ⇄ clubhouse\n         │             tourMap ─► story ─► match ─► results ─► story ─► tourMap … final ─► ending ─► credits ─► title\n         ├─ EXHIBITION ─► teamSelect (join 1–4, sides, teams, park, innings, difficulty) ─► match ─► results\n         ├─ DERBY ─► derbySetup (1–4 players, park, length) ─► derby ─► derbyResults (initials, table)\n         ├─ SPRING TRAINING ─► lessonList ─► lesson ─► lessonList\n         ├─ OPTIONS ─► game.menu('settings') (the engine's screens, with ours added)\n         └─ ABOUT ─► about (About · Source Information · Feedback · Credits)\n\npause menu, on every pausable scene: Resume · Playbook · Pitching Change* · Restart* · Dev Mode* · Settings · Controls ·\nFull screen · Quit to title*      (* hidden in AI Hub sessions; Pitching Change only on defense in a match)\n```\n\nDeep links for tests and checks: `#title`, `#attract`, `#match` (optionally `#match/<park>`), `#derby`, `#training/<n>`,\n`#tour/<stop>`, `#about`, `#gallery` (the animation gallery), `#hubcard` (the hub card with a mock), `#touch`\n(forces the touch-only title).\n\n`menu.items` is given as a function that returns entries, with falsy ones skipped. The engine's pause screen evaluates\nit each time it opens (engine line ~9470: `v(this._game.o.menu.items)` then `.filter(Boolean)`; confirm this in M0 for\nthe standard menu).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The ballpark: units and geometry\n\n**Units.** 16 U (world units) is one tile. Players are `size: 1.25` rigs, about 33 U tall. The game shows distances in\nfeet (`feet = U × 0.32`) and speeds in mph (`mph = U/s × 0.26`). Proportions are arcade, not real: the basepath shows as\n96 ft and center field about 380 ft, which reads right.\n","numLines":300,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GG1fKeD7VA9pvL8YTizDQv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
