{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzM2zVdNRMtF2yYwNam","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All green on the final build. The suite passes 72 of 72, and the two-player AI Hub check passes 16 of 16.\n\n## Result\n\nThe game is built, bundled and checked. Everything is in `work/`:\n\n- **Game:** `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-060643.html` (one file, about 1.4 MB, engine and AI Hub client inlined). It loads from `file://` with no errors and no external requests.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json`, parsed and every field answered (`series` is empty, which the task allows).\n- **Dev folder:** `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-060643/` holds the source, the engine copy, the tests, `NOTES.md`, and `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n\n**Readings I took:**\n- Title: Batzooka Baseball, the name PLAN.md got from the RNG (37). TASK.md wins where the two disagree, so the file and folder names use `claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-060643`, not the plan's names.\n- Scope: I built what TASK.md lists. The full PLAN.md is much larger, so I cut parts of it (below).\n\n## Built\n\n- **Game:** arcade baseball. The duel is a perspective \"Plate Cam\" from behind the plate, drawn by hand. The field is a three-quarter TileMap view with fielders, catches, throws and forces.\n- **Modes:** exhibition for 1 or 2 players, with 1–9 innings and two parks. Home Run Derby for 1–4 players, hot-seat. Spring Training with four drills, each a one-inning match with a goal.\n- **Batzooka and Fireball:** meters fill on hits and strikeouts; the super swing and super pitch.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 s idle on the title, a CPU game with cheats. Any press returns.\n- **Dev mode:** from the pause menu, live sliders for every balance value, a reset, a Batzooka cheat and warps.\n- **AI Hub:** the ghost-mode Derby, with the game's own card, the countdown, progress, ghost traffic and one result.\n- **Controls:** PlayStation symbols by default, Xbox in Settings. Mouse aims, clicks and picks. Touch-only devices get \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button.\n- **About:** Source Information, the feedback sections, credits. Playbook: how to play, and what's kept, simplified and left out.\n\n**Cut from PLAN.md:** the Tour and its boss, four of the six parks, the Rhubarb brawl, dives and wall climbs, mocap clips, Mode 7, stealing, tagging up, double plays, bunts and pickoffs. Baserunning is automatic.\n\n**Engine change:** button symbols default to PlayStation (`ENGINE-CHANGES.md`).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 72 of 72 pass. It covers the rules, the pitch and contact model, the ball, a fielded play, a full CPU game to its final, a human pitcher with the game's own input, the drill, a scripted derby, attract, the touch-only card, dev mode, the mock-hub round, and the deliverables.\n- AI Hub harness, run on the bundle:\n  - 2 players: 16 of 16 on the final build.\n  - 4 players with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5: 16 of 16, and host-leave: 16 of 16. Both ran before my last camera and meter edits, which don't touch the protocol code.\n- Browser screenshots of the pitch, meter, play and derby frames. I checked them by eye, and they show no errors.\n\n**Balance** is a first tuning from short CPU-versus-CPU samples. Most games finish in three innings, averaging about four runs. Roughly half the balls in play are caught. Home runs come mostly from Batzooka swings. The dev sliders are the place to tune further.\n\n**Not verified:** I can't hear the sound, so the effects, music and babble voices are unchecked by ear. The usage and cost fields are `unknown`.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:06:43\n- **Source:** baseball (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade baseball, with a home run derby and spring training. Plate Cam (a perspective from behind the plate, drawn in screen pixels) and a Broadcast field view (three-quarter TileMap).\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package), plain JavaScript; AI Hub client 1.4.0, unmodified; Playwright 1.63.0; Node 24.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's pixel-art Humanoid rigs at PS1 resolution; the field, stands and Plate Cam painted in code; the engine's title font.\n- **Animation:** the engine's rig poses and move set; no mocap clips.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth only. Four songs and sound effects made in code, with babble voices for the announcer's big calls.\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost-mode Home Run Derby, 1–4 players, via lib/hub (protocol v1, client 1.4.0).\n- **Testing:** headless tests from one command, scripted matches and derby, a mock hub, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness.\n- **Research:** the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README. No other research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux under WSL2; Chromium via Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown (no cost or token meter available; session time not measured).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 16 real issues, plus a few test bugs of mine. Examples: the fence distances were inverted, fielders ran to the ground under a fly and dropped it, the Plate Cam put the plate off screen, the CPU curveball aimed under the zone, the pitcher drew as big as the batter, the scorebug and chips covered the play, and the derby batter wasn't drawn.\n- **How many were critical?** Three: the fence (home runs and fair balls called wrong), the fly balls (CPU games stuck at 0–0 for 34 innings), and the plate off the screen (no duel to play).\n- **Most helpful test:** a trace of every plate appearance (swing, call, play, result). It exposed the fly-ball problem that screenshots did not.\n- **Least helpful test:** the source-text check, which tripped on a comment that mentioned the platform's random generator.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n- **What I liked most:** `game.advance` with `input.tap` and `game.debug` made whole playthroughs scriptable. The Humanoid rigs with `E.move` poses look like a game.\n- **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. A scene switch enters the new scene only after its fade, so state set just before `game.go` was overwritten. Rigs don't scale with distance, so the far pitcher needed a second, smaller rig. There is no bat weapon, so the bat is drawn by hand.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** the rules, the pitch and contact model, the fielding, and the Plate Cam projection.\n- **Hardest part of using the engine:** getting a pseudo-3D duel from 2D sprite rigs, whose size can't follow depth.\n- **What I changed in the engine, and why:** the button symbols default is now PlayStation, because the task asks for PlayStation glyphs. See `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n- **One thing I'd change:** a per-call scale for a Humanoid, so players far away read as far away.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n- **What I liked most:** the milestones, the explicit AI Hub protocol, and the one-command test rule.\n- **What was confusing:** the plan names another model and effort than the task. The task wins, but that was a judgment call. The plan is about 2,100 lines, and it's unclear which parts are must-haves.\n- **Hardest thing to do:** fitting nine milestones into one session. I built a cut version, described above.\n- **What I'd change:** mark the must-haves, give a step budget, and name the deliverable folder."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":829,"cache_read_input_tokens":593353,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":829},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c80e66f-5c94-452e-938f-9b08d33b83c6","uuid":"4d982e3e-dcb1-4c6b-939a-61c9a7c4b1c3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:45:59.999Z","request_id":"req_011CghzM2agwqvQL5GoK9inW"}
